# Chapter 1: The Letter. Written from the outline on a blank page by ticket
# 0737 (the story-writing skill, "Rewrites start on a blank page"), after
# 0707's script and the 0724 and 0728 reworks were set aside, and built
# again with different scenes when Nick found the first draft the old
# script's moments in a new voice (2026-10-25).
# Beat sheet: docs/story/chapters/ch01.md. Continuity: docs/story/ledger.md.
# Voice cards: docs/story/characters/*.md.
#
# Scene ids, as chapters/ch01.ron and battles/ch01.ron wire them (0803):
#
#   Before the battle (intro_scenes, in this order)
#     ch01_intro                 the opening captions, then the letter
#     ch01_prebattle             the Red Company; the party gathers
#
#   In the battle (triggers, each once)
#     ch01_first_turn            TurnStart(turn: 1, phase: Player)
#     ch01_boss_engage_lead      CombatStart(unit: "red_captain", against: Some("lead"))
#     ch01_boss_engage_sergeant  CombatStart(unit: "red_captain", against: Some("sergeant"))
#     ch01_boss_engage           CombatStart(unit: "red_captain"): anyone else
#     ch01_boss_half             HalfHp(unit: "red_captain")
#     ch01_death_red_captain     UnitFell(unit: "red_captain")
#     ch01_death_lead            UnitFell(unit: "lead"): game over in both modes
#     ch01_death_<id>            UnitFell(unit: "<id>", mode: Some(Classic))
#     ch01_retreat_<id>          UnitFell(unit: "<id>", mode: Some(Casual))
#       for <id> = retainer, sergeant, poacher, keeper, heretic
#
#   After the battle (victory_scenes, in this order)
#     ch01_victory
#     ch01_tbc
#
# The lead has three reply choices: one each in ch01_intro, ch01_prebattle
# and ch01_victory. The objective is Defeat Harl (0845): Tamsin says the
# rest of the company runs when he falls, and no line counts the enemy
# (the map shows them).
#
# The title cinematic (assets/cinematics/title.ron) shows the first three
# boxes of ch01_intro (the opening captions) and the first box of
# ch01_prebattle (Maud's news). Keep those boxes first.
#
# Tone (docs/design/setting-and-tone.md, "Where the jokes go"; the
# story-writing skill, Craft): the opening and the battle are played
# straight, which means no comedy bits, not no warmth. The care is on the
# body (a shoulder, wet clothes, bread, a hand). The lead's wry reply may
# get a light answer, briefly. Punctuation is the voice acting: Tamsin and
# Harl raise their voices, Maud breaks off, Aske ends flat, Hollis trails
# off once a scene at most.
#
# Expressions: the plain build has no portrait for anyone but the lead
# (the cast's are bought art, assets-private/), so this script uses only
# the five every character has: neutral, happy, angry, sad, surprised.
#
# A companion who fell in Classic is gone (0716; @if blocks, 0715,
# ADR-0055). ch01_victory plays without any of the five, and without all
# of them: the order's seal, that only the new lord may use it, and the
# road to the hall reach the player on every path. Read it with one
# companion gone, and with only the lead left, before changing it.
# docs/story/ledger.md ("If a companion died in Chapter 1") records what
# each death leaves unsaid.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Before the battle
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The yard behind the rented house, at dawn (harrowby_yard.map). An
# ordinary morning with the letter unread in Hollis's coat: a boot, the
# sea he has never seen, what the lead will do when the exile lifts. The
# bell rings early, and he sends the lead for the swords.

@scene ch01_intro
@music village_home
> Three years after {n:event.ash_peace}. The war is over. The soldiers who fought it were never paid.
> Some went home. Some are still on the roads, in their old companies, under their old captains.
> In {n:place.thornmarch}, at the far edge of the kingdom, an exile chops wood.
@caption {N:place.harrowby}, dawn
@place harrowby_yard 12,8
@opening ch01_sparring
@left lead neutral
@right retainer neutral
> {n:retainer.first} sits on the block by the fire with one of {lead}'s boots on his knee and an awl in his fist. He has not slept. There is a folded paper in his coat that he keeps touching.
retainer: Sideways, with the grain. You'll have the axe in your foot, and then who mends the boot? Give me the other one.
retainer: Forty years on my feet in other people's boots. Never seen the sea. Did you know that? Forty years. Pass me the thread.
lead: Why the sea?
retainer[sad]: Because it's the one thing the boy wrote home about from the war that I believed. ...There. Try that. And what'll you do, when this lifts? It lifts. Kings forget.
@choice
* earnest: Stay. Fix the mill right.
  retainer[happy]: The mill. Aye. Somebody should, before it falls in the river.
* wry: Beat you without you letting me.
  retainer: Letting you. Hm. Put the boot on.
* blunt: Go home.
  retainer[sad]: ...Aye. Well. Put the boot on.
@endchoice
> {lead} puts the boot on. Across the hamlet the chapel bell starts, an hour early, and goes on ringing.
retainer[neutral]: That's not the hour. Get the swords. Under the floor, you know where. Go on.
@end

# By the chapel, minutes later (ch01.map, where the party starts). The
# paper on the door; Hollis takes the letter out in front of everyone and
# the lead names the hand; Rue burns it; the plan.

@scene ch01_prebattle
@caption {N:place.harrowby}, by the chapel
@place battle 3,12
@music dungeon_tense
@left lead neutral
@right keeper surprised
keeper: This was nailed to the chapel door. I took it down before anyone-- I took it down, and I rang the bell. They'll burn the village at noon unless three people walk up to the toll-fort.
keeper: "The exile. The old man. The girl with the burned hands." ...That's not how a soldier says it. That's how a keeper writes it down. ...Go on, somebody else read it. My hands won't.
@right sergeant angry
sergeant: Give it to me, Keeper, before you drop it. That's {n:red_captain.first}'s writing on somebody else's paper, he writes on the backs of things. And he'll do it. He burned a farm last winter for less.
@right heretic angry
heretic: I'm the one on the paper, and I'll go up. On my own feet, with my hands out of my sleeves.
@right sergeant neutral
sergeant: What do you have to do with any of this, Sparks? With their mill, with any of it?
@right heretic angry
heretic: Why don't you ask whoever wrote it?
@right retainer neutral
> {n:retainer.first} takes the folded paper out of his coat and holds it out, to nobody in particular.
retainer: This came last night. By peddler. It says get out of the march by tonight, they're coming for everyone who was at the mill that night, burn this. It isn't signed.
@right sergeant neutral
sergeant: Whose hand, Captain? ...It's the boy's, isn't it. Your boy's.
lead: {n:rival.first}'s.
@right retainer sad
retainer: Aye. ...Burn it. Somebody. I can't.
@right heretic neutral
> {n:heretic} takes it out of his hand, and it is ash between her fingers before anyone can speak. She is looking at {lead}, not at the letter.
@right sergeant neutral
sergeant: Sparks. Your sleeve's smoking.
@right heretic neutral
heretic: It does that.
@right poacher neutral
poacher: Nobody goes up. Not you, noble, not her. They shot three of my deer from the wall last night, ate one raw and left two. Men who cannot wait to cook a deer will not wait for noon.
@right retainer neutral
retainer: Then we don't wait either. The ford's the only road out, and they're sitting on it. Keeper, the whole hamlet goes in the chapel. We have steel.
@choice
* earnest: Nobody goes up to that fort but us.
  @right keeper surprised
  keeper: Thank you. No-- I mean-- yes. Go. {N:god.mother.name} keep you, all of you. I'll get them inside.
  @right retainer neutral
* wry: He wants three of us. I'm flattered.
  @right sergeant happy
  sergeant: Don't make me laugh, Your Former Grace. Not this morning. Where's my horse.
  @right retainer neutral
* blunt: We go now. Before they eat.
  @right poacher surprised
  poacher: Yes. Now. You listened to me. Nobody listens to me.
  @right retainer neutral
@endchoice
@end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# In the battle
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

@scene ch01_first_turn
@left retainer neutral
retainer: Kid. Boots holding? Good. Keep the road under you. The fort's not going anywhere.
@end

# Harl's first fight. A scene written for him and one character replaces
# the general one (docs/design/battle-scenes-and-recruitment.md).

@scene ch01_boss_engage
@right red_captain neutral
red_captain: Forty men I brought home from a war, and the crown gave us a road. Go home, whoever you are, before I take it out on you.
@end

@scene ch01_boss_engage_lead
@left lead neutral
@right red_captain neutral
red_captain: There you are, Your Grace. You could have walked up like the paper said. Three hundred a head, somebody's paying for you. Hold still and earn it.
@end

@scene ch01_boss_engage_sergeant
@left sergeant angry
@right red_captain neutral
red_captain: Who's that on the gray? ...{n:sergeant.last}? {n:sergeant.last}, you're alive. I had you down as dead in a ditch.
sergeant: You had me down as a lot of things. Get off the wall, {n:red_captain.first}.
red_captain[angry]: Come up and fetch me! I put you on your first horse, girl. I can put you off it.
sergeant[sad]: You did. And I held the horses at that farm after, and watched. ...Get off the wall.
@end

@scene ch01_boss_half
@right red_captain angry
red_captain: Should've cooked the deer. Should've waited for-- Come on, then! I'm still standing.
@end

# Said to the lead, who may be nowhere near him. He hands over the one
# thing he knows (the seal, the silver) because it hurts, then pleads for
# his men.

@scene ch01_death_red_captain
@left lead neutral
@right red_captain sad
red_captain: Don't hang the boys. They were hungry, that's all they-- Your Grace. Your Grace, come here.
red_captain: A lord bought you. Your own wax on the paper, your own crest, silver up front. Ask the new one in your hall what you cost him.
@end

# The lead's fall is game over in both modes.

@scene ch01_death_lead
@left lead sad
> {lead} is down. In {n:place.harrowby} the bell goes on ringing for a while, and then it stops.
@end

# The companions: a death quote in Classic, a retreat line in Casual
# (docs/design/death-and-difficulty.md). Last words are about the living;
# the shapes differ on purpose. They can fire in any order, so none
# speaks to another companion by name.

@scene ch01_death_retainer
@left retainer sad
retainer: Kid-- no. Don't. ...The sea. I'd have liked to see it. Tell the boy that, if you see him. Not the rest.
@end

@scene ch01_retreat_retainer
@left retainer angry
retainer: My knee. The other one, this time. I'm not hurt, I'm old. Fill the gap.
@end

@scene ch01_death_sergeant
@left sergeant sad
sergeant: Stand in front of me. Whoever's nearest, stand in front, I won't be seen like this. ...The mare goes to whoever feeds her. She'll pick.
@end

@scene ch01_retreat_sergeant
@left sergeant angry
sergeant: Down. I'm down, I'm not out. Help me up-- no, don't, I'll do it. Ride on.
@end

@scene ch01_death_poacher
@left poacher sad
poacher: They had a third man on the wall. I did not see him. ...Skin the deer properly, before it turns. Noble. I did not see him.
@end

@scene ch01_retreat_poacher
@left poacher angry
poacher: I cannot draw. Give me a sword. I have never held a sword. Give me one anyway.
@end

@scene ch01_death_keeper
@left keeper sad
keeper: The bell. Somebody ring the bell when it's over, or they won't come out of the chapel. They won't come out until they hear it. Promise me the bell.
@end

@scene ch01_retreat_keeper
@left keeper surprised
keeper: Oh. Oh, that's a lot of blood, and it's mine, isn't it. I'm going to sit down now. Leave me, I can do my own hands.
@end

@scene ch01_death_heretic
@left heretic sad
heretic: I burned it for the old man and he didn't even-- never mind. ...It isn't noon yet. It isn't noon, so why is it dark?
@end

@scene ch01_retreat_heretic
@left heretic angry
heretic: Enough. I'm out, I'm empty, don't-- I'll be in the ditch. Don't look for me.
@end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# After the battle
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The toll-fort, just below its keep at (20, 2). Rue finds the order and
# the lead answers her; Hollis sits down with his back to everyone; Tamsin
# says it for him, then goes to the body; Aske feeds everyone; Rue sets
# the road; Hollis digs.

@scene ch01_victory
@caption The toll-fort, before noon
@place battle 20,3
@music scene_sad
> The gate stands open. What was left of {n:faction.red_company} went out through it at a run when {n:red_captain.first} fell. Inside: a cold fire, a half-skinned deer, and {n:red_captain.first}.
@left lead neutral
@if heretic
@right heretic neutral
> {n:heretic} is going through the dead men's packs for food. In {n:red_captain.first}'s coat she finds a paper: three lines, a price, a seal in blue wax. She stops with it in both hands.
lead: That's the {n:family.veyne} seal.
heretic[angry]: Yours. The one you cut off your coat. ...The last time I saw this wax it was on a cart, at night, and the cart was full of children. Your house's wax. Did you know?
@choice
* earnest: No. And I'll find out who did.
  heretic: Then find out. I'll be watching while you do.
* wry: Carts? I chopped wood.
  heretic: Somebody chopped wood. Somebody stamped the passes. Fine, heir.
* blunt: Say what you mean.
  heretic: I did. ...Keep the paper. No. Give it here. I'll keep it.
@endchoice
@right clear
@else
> In {n:red_captain.first}'s coat, a folded paper sealed in blue wax: the crest of {n:house.veyne}, the one {lead} cut off {their} own coat.
@choice
* earnest: We go and ask him. To his face.
  > Nobody argues.
* wry: Outlaw. It has a ring to it.
  > Nobody laughs.
* blunt: {N:place.veyne_hall}. Tonight.
  > Nobody says otherwise.
@endchoice
@endif
@if retainer
@right retainer sad
> {n:retainer.first} looks at the wax over {lead}'s shoulder for a long moment. Then he goes and sits down on the gate step with his back to everyone.
@right clear
@endif
@if sergeant
@right sergeant angry
@if retainer
sergeant: Don't look at him. Give him a minute. ...It's the boy's, Your Former Grace. The hall, the title, the wax, it all went to him with the king's thanks. You knew that. You just never saw it in wax.
@else
sergeant: It's the boy's, Your Former Grace. The Captain's boy, {n:rival.first}, who wrote the letter. The hall, the title, the wax, all his with the king's thanks. The Captain never had to see it.
@endif
> She goes to the body, cuts the red cloth off his head with her knife, and doesn't look at his face. She takes out the ledger, writes one word in it, and shuts it.
@if keeper
sergeant[sad]: He picked me out of a muster line once. Fourteen of us. ...Keeper. How do you spell "fourteen"?
@else
sergeant[sad]: He picked me out of a muster line once. Fourteen of us. ...I can't spell "fourteen". It'll have to be the number.
@endif
@right clear
@else
> Only the one who holds the hall seals with it, and the hall is {n:rival}'s now. Nobody goes to the body.
@endif
@if poacher
@right poacher neutral
poacher: Sit down. All of you. Nobody has eaten since the bell. Sit. I will do the deer. You will only make it worse.
@right clear
@endif
@if keeper
@right keeper sad
@if heretic
keeper: I'll write to {n:place.greywater} and tell them she's no danger-- ...No. I can't write to the Abbey, can I. Not ever again. And past the stone they hang you for that coat. Let me see your hands.
@else
keeper: I'll write to {n:place.greywater} and tell them that-- No. I can't write to the Abbey, can I. Not ever again. And past the boundary stone they hang you for that coat. Give me your hand.
@endif
@right clear
@endif
@if heretic
@right heretic angry
heretic: This seal belongs to a hall. Everybody knows where {n:place.veyne_hall} is. When the deer's eaten I'm going there, with this. Alone, if I have to.
lead: Not alone.
heretic: Fine. In front of me, then. Where I can see you.
@right clear
@endif
@if retainer
@right retainer neutral
> {n:retainer.first} gets up off the step. He takes {lead}'s sword, cleans the blade on his own sleeve, and gives it back. He says nothing about the hall, or the boy.
@if sergeant
@else
retainer[sad]: {n:sergeant.first} owed me two crowns at dice and brought it up every week like I'd forget. Her horse is still standing saddled at the gate. Somebody see to it.
@endif
@if keeper
@else
retainer[sad]: The Keeper couldn't lie and couldn't play dice, and sat down to lose every week anyway. I'll dig hers deep. By the chapel wall.
@endif
@if heretic
@else
retainer[sad]: The girl. Twice this week she took bread off my table and glared at me for noticing. She was next to me on that paper, and I don't know her family to tell them.
@endif
@if poacher
@else
retainer[sad]: {n:poacher} drilled worse than any recruit I ever had, and came back every morning to be told so. She faces north. See she faces north.
@endif
@right clear
@else
> {lead} digs the graves by the chapel {themself}, one by one. Nobody digs one for {n:red_captain.first}.
@endif
> Down in {n:place.harrowby}, somebody has found the bell rope. It's ringing again.
@end

# The old lord's study, at night (veyne_study.map). Two letters written
# before the news; Dace hands over the lie and locks the other away.

@scene ch01_tbc
@caption {N:place.veyne_hall}
@place veyne_study 12,7
@music talk_antagonist
@opening ch01_pacing
> Night. The old lord's study. Two sealed letters on the desk, written before the news. The news, an hour old: {n:red_captain.nickname} dead in the toll-fort, the exile walking north with five others.
@left rival neutral
@right messenger neutral
messenger: My lord. {N:vowmaster.title} asks whether it is done. He asked me to bring your answer in writing.
> {n:rival.first} looks at the two letters for as long as it takes to breathe in, and hands him the one on the left.
rival: It's done. That says so. Go.
@right clear
> When the door shuts he locks the other letter in the desk drawer, still sealed, on top of the seal, and sits down in the old lord's chair, which he never does when anyone can see.
rival[happy]: You never once did as you were told, {n:family.veyne}. Your whole life. ...Good.
@end
